---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';

const description =
  'Dastan Aitzhanov on building AI systems, allocating capital, and the governance questions that connect both.';
---

<Base title="About" description={description}>
  <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
  <div class="prose about-prose">
    <p>
      I build AI systems, and I allocate capital. They have never felt like separate kinds of work.
    </p>

    <h2>Technical work</h2>
    <p>
      The technical thread runs through cloud infrastructure, data platforms, and machine learning.
      I spent nearly eight years as a solutions architect at SparkCognition, Amazon Web Services,
      Snowflake, and Databricks, advising Fortune 500 companies in manufacturing, construction,
      healthcare, and logistics. The problems I liked were practical ones:
      getting data to a place a model could use, making a system reliable enough that someone would
      trust it on an ordinary day, and finding which part of a stack was actually doing the work.
    </p>

    <h2>Current inquiry</h2>
    <p>
      In August 2026 I left Databricks to build a company, still in stealth, around agentic work,
      local inference, and distributed execution. The question underneath those experiments is what happens when an AI system moves
      from producing information to exercising delegated authority: what it may see, what it may
      decide, what it may do, and how that authority can be withdrawn.
    </p>

    <h2>Ownership</h2>
    <p>
      Before I worked in technology, I ran the commercial real estate business of a holding company
      in Kazakhstan through the 2008 financial crisis. I then founded a quantitative investment firm
      in the United States and co-invested as a limited partner in Texas hotel and commercial real
      estate deals. Ownership changes what you notice. A forecast can be right while the operation is still wrong.
      Incentives can produce activity without producing value. The person who authorizes a decision
      still carries the consequence after the analysis is complete.
    </p>
    <p>
      That lens shapes how I think about AI. As analysis becomes cheaper and systems become more
      capable of acting, legitimate authority becomes more important, not less.
    </p>

    <h2>Direction</h2>
    <p>
      I do not expect policy to replace my technical work. I expect governance to become part of the
      job of building increasingly capable AI systems. I write here to make that investigation public
      when I have something definite enough to defend.
    </p>
    <p>I live in Texas.</p>
    <p>
      Email is the way to reach me:
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      Code I have published lives on <a href={site.github} rel="me">GitHub</a>, and selected public
      work is collected on the <a href="/work/">Work page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
